<header><p class="st-kicker">princely state · Indian princely states</p><h1 class="st-title">Shahpura State</h1><p class="st-meta">1629 CE – 1948 CE (approx.) · capital Shahpura</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A small Sisodia Rajput state of Rajasthan, centre of the Ramsnehi sect and home of the painted Phad scrolls.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Shahpura was granted in 1629 by the Mughal emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shah-jahan">Shah Jahan</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sujan-singh-of-shahpura">Sujan Singh</a>, of the Ranawat branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sisodia">Sisodia</a> house of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/udaipur-state">Mewar</a>. The town is named after the emperor. The rulers also held lands from Mewar, which created a long dispute over their status.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Rajadhirajs ruled a small territory near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhilwara">Bhilwara</a>. Shahpura became the headquarters of the Ramsnehi devotional sect, founded in the 18th century, and the home of the Joshi painters of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phad-painting">Phad</a>, long cloth scrolls used by bards narrating the epics of folk deities.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Shahpura joined the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-state-of-rajasthan">United State of Rajasthan</a> in 1948. The town, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhilwara">Bhilwara</a> district, remains a Ramsnehi pilgrimage centre and the heart of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phad-painting">Phad painting</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/shahpura-state.jpg" alt="Shahpura State" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Robert Alfers · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Shahpura.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1629 CE – 1948 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>princely state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Shahpura</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Sujan Singh</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Mewari</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Shahpura</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1788&amp;focus=shahpura-state" aria-label="Shahpura State on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6663 1235 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Shahpura State lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6983 1491L7018 1385M6983 1491L7364 1601M6983 1491L7364 1602M6983 1491L6996 1446"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7018 1385h0M7364 1601h0M7364 1602h0M6996 1446h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6983 1491h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6983 1491h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1788&amp;focus=shahpura-state">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
